-- APPLY-ONE, the NEGATIVE twin of `examples/apply_one_descriptor_proof.duo`.
-- c0 §43 `law.brace` row 1, §44 `apply.carries`. gap[092].
--
-- WHY THIS TWIN EXISTS, and why the neither-case twin does not cover it.
-- `apply_one_neither.duo` pins the third outcome: a subject in NEITHER home.
-- This pins the first outcome REFUSING — the subject resolves, it is a
-- descriptor, and the pack hands it a label it has no field for.
--
-- That distinction is the whole benefit of resolving the subject instead of
-- calling it, and without this row the resolution could regress into a silent
-- success. Before gap[092], `point{ x = 1.0, z = 2.0 }` was an application of a
-- name nothing declared, and if the emitter had merely been taught to build a
-- table, `z` would have become a table KEY: no diagnostic, a `point` with a
-- field the descriptor never declared, and `q.z` reading it back. A construction
-- that accepts any label is not construction, it is a table with a name on it.
--
-- MEASURED 2026-08-09:
--
--     error: descriptor 'point' has no field 'z', so the applied pack carries
--            a label the subject cannot receive
--
-- Pinned on the substring `has no field` plus the label `z`, so rewording the
-- sentence does not move the fixture.

point: @{ x: f64, y: f64 }

main(): void
    q = point{ x = 1.0, z = 2.0 }
    print(q.x)

